WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.975
(whimsical music)

00:00:01.975 --> 00:00:04.372
- [Narrator] This mosque's
distinct red and white patterns

00:00:04.372 --> 00:00:05.802
and tall minarets

00:00:05.802 --> 00:00:09.969
can be seen from every part
of this Sri Lankan town.

00:00:14.051 --> 00:00:17.209
Jami Ul-Alfar, known
locally a the 'Red Mosque',

00:00:17.209 --> 00:00:20.097
is located in Colombo, Sri Lanka.

00:00:20.097 --> 00:00:21.755
It was commissioned in 1908

00:00:21.755 --> 00:00:23.969
by Indian Muslims who
traveled for business

00:00:23.969 --> 00:00:28.794
and needed a place of
worship while in Sri Lanka.

00:00:28.794 --> 00:00:31.294
The designer and builder
was H.L. Saibu Labbe,

00:00:31.294 --> 00:00:34.124
and the original structure
only took one year to build

00:00:34.124 --> 00:00:36.613
and was completed in 1909.

00:00:36.613 --> 00:00:39.485
He was influenced by
Indo-Saracenic architecture,

00:00:39.485 --> 00:00:42.407
which is a hybrid of
Indian and Islamic styles.

00:00:42.407 --> 00:00:43.657
The best example of this

00:00:43.657 --> 00:00:47.275
is the mosque's pomegranate-shaped domes.

00:00:47.275 --> 00:00:51.347
The mosque started small; only
500 to 1,000 people gathered.

00:00:51.347 --> 00:00:54.284
But it has since grown and
expanded to be six floors

00:00:54.284 --> 00:00:57.617
to accommodate around 10,000 worshipers.

00:00:58.655 --> 00:01:01.675
Apart from being a major tourist
destination for visitors,

00:01:01.675 --> 00:01:04.613
Jami Ul-Alfar is also
the main place of worship

00:01:04.613 --> 00:01:06.530
for Sri Lankan Muslims.

